<section class="hero" id="home"><div class="wrap hero-content"><div class="hero-kicker">A cultural journey · 75 years and beyond</div><h1>A TASTE OF INDIA<span>THE LIVING CANVAS</span></h1><p class="hero-sub">Art, culture and identity — from our roots to our future.</p><a class="btn" href="#timeline">Explore India’s Cultural Journey <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><div class="hero-note">A century in the making <span>·</span> A future still being painted</div></section>
<div class="intro-strip"><div class="wrap"><span>Many traditions, one evolving canvas</span><span>✳ &nbsp;Memory is a living practice&nbsp; ✳</span><span>India through time, made by people</span></div></div>
<section class="section timeline" id="timeline"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><span class="eyebrow">01 / The long view</span><h2>Culture is never still.</h2><p>Move across the timeline to trace how art and identity change with the lives around them.</p></div><div class="timeline-line" role="tablist" aria-label="Cultural timeline"><button class="time-item" role="tab" aria-selected="true" data-period="roots"><span class="time-dot"></span><strong>Pre-1947</strong><small>Many inheritances</small></button><button class="time-item" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-period="independence"><span class="time-dot"></span><strong>1947</strong><small>A threshold</small></button><button class="time-item" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-period="post"><span class="time-dot"></span><strong>Post-Independence</strong><small>New institutions</small></button><button class="time-item" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-period="today"><span class="time-dot"></span><strong>Today</strong><small>Tradition × technology</small></button><button class="time-item" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-period="future"><span class="time-dot"></span><strong>2047</strong><small>Many possible futures</small></button></div><div class="timeline-detail" aria-live="polite"><h3 id="timeline-title">Deep roots, many voices</h3><div class="timeline-copy" id="timeline-copy"><p>For centuries, the Indian subcontinent has been a meeting ground of communities, languages, faiths and artistic practices. Its cultural history is a network of regional stories rather than one single tradition.</p><p>Visual expression ranges from ancient rock art and temple sculpture to miniature painting, folk forms and textiles. Dance, music and literature developed through courtly, devotional, community and everyday settings.</p><p>Trade, migration and changing patrons carried ideas across regions and beyond the subcontinent. Artists and craftspeople adapted what they encountered, making continuity and change part of the same story.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="section" id="roots"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><span class="eyebrow">02 / Roots</span><h2>A thousand ways to make meaning.</h2><p>India’s cultural inheritance is not one tradition but a conversation among regions, communities, languages and generations.</p></div><div class="roots-grid"><article class="root-card"><span class="num">01 — FORM</span><h3>Visual arts</h3><p>From cave murals and miniature painting to temple sculpture, folk painting and living studio practice.</p></article><article class="root-card"><span class="num">02 — MOVEMENT</span><h3>Dance</h3><p>Classical forms such as Bharatanatyam and Kathak alongside community and folk dances that mark place and season.</p></article><article class="root-card"><span class="num">03 — SOUND</span><h3>Music</h3><p>Hindustani and Carnatic systems, devotional song, regional instruments and oral traditions, each with its own grammar.</p></article><article class="root-card"><span class="num">04 — PLACE</span><h3>Architecture</h3><p>Stepwells, temples, mosques, forts, havelis and homes shaped by climate, craft, belief and exchange.</p></article><article class="root-card"><span class="num">05 — WORD</span><h3>Literature</h3><p>Epic and classical works, Bhakti and Sufi poetry, and a vast constellation of writing across Indian languages.</p></article><article class="root-card"><span class="num">06 — THREAD</span><h3>Textiles</h3><p>Weaving, embroidery, dyeing and printing carry local knowledge in patterns made to be worn and shared.</p></article><article class="root-card"><span class="num">07 — HAND</span><h3>Handicrafts</h3><p>Pottery, metalwork, wood carving, toys and countless other crafts keep material knowledge in motion.</p></article><article class="root-card"><span class="num">08 — EXCHANGE</span><h3>Living traditions</h3><p>These forms adapt through migration, trade, patronage and everyday creativity—not behind glass, but in use.</p></article></div><p class="roots-note">A starting point, not a complete catalogue. Every region holds its own histories and forms.</p></div></section>
<section class="section independence" id="1947"><div class="wrap independence-grid"><div class="year-card"><span class="eyebrow" style="color:#efd29b">A turning point</span><div class="big">1947</div><p>Freedom opened a new chapter. Partition left a wound that art would keep trying to name.</p></div><div class="ind-copy"><span class="eyebrow">03 / Independence & Partition</span><h2>A nation imagined, remade, remembered.</h2><p>Independence brought new questions: how could a diverse people imagine a shared future? Partition forced millions from home and divided families, places and cultural worlds. Artists, writers and filmmakers responded to both possibility and loss.</p><p>New cultural institutions—including the Sahitya Akademi (1954), Lalit Kala Akademi (1954) and Sangeet Natak Akademi (1953)—supported arts across languages and disciplines. In cinema, literature and visual culture, artists argued over what modern Indian identity might mean.</p><div class="factline"><span><b>1953</b> Sangeet Natak Akademi established</span><span><b>1954</b> Lalit Kala & Sahitya Akademi established</span></div></div></div></section>
<section class="section today" id="today"><div class="wrap"><div class="today-top"><div class="section-head"><span class="eyebrow">04 / Today</span><h2>Tradition × technology.</h2><p>Today’s culture moves between inherited knowledge and new tools. Pick a lens to see some of the ways they meet.</p></div><div class="modes" role="group" aria-label="Choose a contemporary culture theme"><button class="mode-btn" aria-pressed="true" data-mode="digital">Digital art</button><button class="mode-btn" aria-pressed="false" data-mode="music">Fusion music</button><button class="mode-btn" aria-pressed="false" data-mode="fashion">Fashion</button><button class="mode-btn" aria-pressed="false" data-mode="cinema">Cinema</button></div></div><div class="today-feature"><div class="today-image" role="img" aria-label="Artist painting a colorful canvas"></div><div class="today-text" aria-live="polite"><span class="eyebrow" id="today-kicker">New tools, old questions</span><h3 id="today-title">Art goes online.</h3><p id="today-copy">Artists share work directly, remix visual languages and build new audiences. Digital tools can widen access while raising questions about credit, ownership and who gets seen.</p><div class="tag-row" id="today-tags"><span class="tag">Digital art</span><span class="tag">Social media</span><span class="tag">New audiences</span></div></div></div><p style="color:#777268;font-size:.76rem;margin-top:15px">Also reshaping culture: streaming cinema, online archives and digital heritage preservation projects.</p></div></section>
<section class="section future" id="2047"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><span class="eyebrow">05 / The next canvas</span><h2>What could 2047 hold?</h2><p>A centenary invites imagination. These are possible directions, shaped by choices that people and communities are making now.</p></div><div class="future-grid"><article class="future-card"><span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">✳</span><h3>AI as a creative partner</h3><p>Artists may use AI to explore patterns and forms while centring human intention, authorship and consent.</p></article><article class="future-card"><span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">⌁</span><h3>Culture in AR & VR</h3><p>Immersive experiences could bring historic sites and performance traditions to people across distances.</p></article><article class="future-card"><span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">◎</span><h3>Living digital archives</h3><p>Community-led preservation may help safeguard languages, recordings and craft knowledge for future generations.</p></article><article class="future-card"><span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">↗</span><h3>Global Indian culture</h3><p>More exchange across diasporas and borders could create new collaborations and hybrid cultural forms.</p></article><article class="future-card"><span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">❋</span><h3>Sustainable traditions</h3><p>Renewed interest in repair, natural materials and local knowledge could inform lower-impact creative practice.</p></article><article class="future-card"><span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">⌘</span><h3>More people making</h3><p>Accessible tools may open new routes to create and share, if infrastructure and opportunity are widely available.</p></article></div><div class="future-disclaimer"><strong>Possibilities, not predictions.</strong> These ideas describe futures people might build—not facts about what will happen by 2047.</div></div></section>
<section class="section explore" id="explore"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><span class="eyebrow">06 / Explore</span><h2>Make the connections yourself.</h2><p>Choose an activity: explore regional traditions, compare visual eras, imagine a future or test what you remember.</p></div><div class="explore-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Explore activities"><button class="explore-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="true" data-panel="map">Explore India</button><button class="explore-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-panel="compare">Then vs Now</button><button class="explore-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-panel="build">Build India 2047</button><button class="explore-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-panel="quiz">Cultural quiz</button></div>
