const timelineData={roots:['Deep roots, many voices',['For centuries, the subcontinent has been a meeting ground of communities, languages, faiths and artistic practices. Its cultural history is a network of regional stories rather than one single tradition.','Visual expression ranges from prehistoric rock art and ancient murals to temple sculpture, miniature painting, folk forms and textiles. Dance, music and literature developed in courtly, devotional, community and everyday settings.','Trade, migration and changing patrons carried ideas across regions and beyond the subcontinent. Artists and craftspeople adapted what they encountered, making continuity and change part of the same story.','Many forms were learned through practice: songs memorised, patterns woven, gestures taught by a guru, and craft knowledge shared within families and communities. These roots are living knowledge, not a frozen past.']],independence:['A threshold of freedom and loss',['On 15 August 1947, India became independent after a long anticolonial movement. Freedom opened the work of imagining a democratic nation across an extraordinary range of languages, regions and faiths.','Partition accompanied independence with immense violence and displacement. Families crossed new borders, homes and neighbourhoods were lost, and artists have continued to grapple with memory, migration and belonging.','Writers and visual artists made room for grief and difficult questions; cinema and theatre became powerful spaces to represent social change and public life. Their responses were diverse and sometimes sharply contested.','The years that followed brought institutions intended to support the arts and letters, helping create new stages for debates about what modern Indian culture could be.']],post:['Institutions, experiments, new publics',['In the decades after independence, national academies, museums, universities and public broadcasters expanded support and visibility for creative work. Regional institutions and local initiatives were equally important to cultural life.','Artists worked across inherited and modern forms. Painters debated abstraction and representation; theatre-makers and writers explored social change, while filmmakers developed new ways to tell stories about everyday India.','The growth of print, radio, cinema and later television brought music, literature and performance to larger audiences. These media also changed who could participate in cultural conversations.','There was no single post-independence style. The period held both celebration and critique, with artists negotiating patronage, identity, inequality and the possibilities of a plural public culture.']],today:['Tradition meets new tools',['Today, a classical dancer may share rehearsal clips online while training within a lineage; a weaver may find new buyers through digital platforms. Old and new practices often coexist in the same creative life.','Independent cinema and streaming have widened the reach of work in many languages, even as questions about discoverability, fair pay and representation remain.','Social media lets artists find audiences directly and lets communities document cultural practices. It also brings difficult questions about credit, consent, misinformation and the commercial use of tradition.','Digital heritage projects can preserve recordings, texts and images, but preservation works best when the people whose culture is represented help decide what is shared and how it is described.']],future:['A future still being made',['In 2047, India will mark one hundred years of independence. The cultural future will be shaped by choices people make now—in schools, studios, communities, institutions and online spaces.','AI-assisted tools could help artists explore new forms, while raising urgent questions about attribution, training data, consent and the value of human creative work.','Augmented and virtual reality might let people encounter distant sites or performances in new ways. Access, context and respect for living communities will matter as much as technical novelty.','A more sustainable creative future could value repair, local materials, living languages and fair livelihoods. These are possibilities to imagine and build, not predictions or guaranteed outcomes.']]};document.querySelectorAll('.time-item').forEach(btn=>btn.addEventListener('click',()=>{document.querySelectorAll('.time-item').forEach(b=>b.setAttribute('aria-selected','false'));btn.setAttribute('aria-selected','true');const d=timelineData[btn.dataset.period];document.querySelector('#timeline-title').textContent=d[0];document.querySelector('#timeline-copy').innerHTML=d[1].map(p=>'<p>'+p+'</p>').join('')}));
